-- name    : X25519Dalek_PlacedObj
-- status  : Definition
-- author  : @dbenbenn
-- created : 2026-11-22T21:15:53.570849+00:00
-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/33c9f1d1-6221-4be4-b61b-a2d5aa7f4484
-- title:
--   x25519-dalek's X25519 compiled for AArch64, as a multi-block placed library
-- statement:
--   The object code of the two x25519-dalek 3.0.0 functions that libsignal calls, `StaticSecret::diffie_hellman` and `PublicKey::from(&StaticSecret)`, together with every function and read-only constant of x25519-dalek, curve25519-dalek 5.0.0, subtle 2.6.1 and Rust's core library that they reach, as a placed library in the `Arm64` semantics (google-deepmind/code-verification's `Arm64.Trusted`, published as the `Def_Arm64_*` bundles).
--
--   **Provenance.** The bytes are DeepMind's committed object bundle `CryptoX25519PlacedObj.bin` (39,308 bytes) at google-deepmind/code-verification commit `8c8d371f`, extracted from the rustc 1.98.1 (`aarch64-unknown-linux-musl`) build of libsignal; DeepMind checked it against the linked `libsignal-cli` executable. Their module `PlacedObjDefs.lean` is published here with its 22 byte slices inlined as literal lists (the `include_bytes%` elaborator is metaprogramming, which prove2.me rejects), its imports renamed to the published bundles, the namespace renamed to `X25519Dalek`, and the opcode-exclusion theorems at its end dropped (the file's notice says so).
--
--   **The library `x25519CliMultiLib`.**
--   - 19 code blocks `x25519CliBlk00` … `x25519CliBlk18` (2,127 instructions, 8,508 bytes), one per function, each with its symbol and the relocations the linker resolves: calls between blocks, and the addresses of the read-only constants.
--   - 3 read-only data objects: `FieldElement51::ONE` (`x25519CliData00`, 40 bytes), the constant $121666$ as a `FieldElement51` (`x25519CliData01`, 40 bytes) and `ED25519_BASEPOINT_TABLE` (`x25519CliData02`, 30,720 bytes).
--   - 2 exports: the two x25519-dalek functions, `StaticSecret::diffie_hellman` (x25519-dalek 3.0.0, src/x25519.rs, lines 210–211, p. 7 of the excerpts: “Perform a Diffie-Hellman key agreement between `self` and `their_public` key to produce a `SharedSecret`.”) and `PublicKey::from(&StaticSecret)` (line 256: “Given an x25519 [`StaticSecret`] key, compute its corresponding [`PublicKey`].”). The first calls curve25519-dalek's `MontgomeryPoint::mul_clamped` (src/montgomery.rs, lines 148–149, p. 8: “Multiply this point by `clamp_integer(bytes)`. For a description of clamping, see [`clamp_integer`].”).
--
--   The ladder's loop, `MontgomeryPoint::mul_bits_be`, is inlined into block 15 (`Mul<&Scalar> for &MontgomeryPoint`); curve25519-dalek 5.0.0, src/montgomery.rs, lines 176–177 (p. 8 of the excerpts), documents it: “Given `self` $= u_0(P)$, and a big-endian bit representation of an integer $n$, return $u_0([n]P)$. This is constant time in the length of `bits`.”
--
--   That the blocks are the compiled Rust functions whose symbols they carry rests on the provenance above, not on any statement.
--
--   Three relocation targets are neither blocks nor data objects: Rust's `panic_bounds_check` and two anonymous read-only constants whose addresses are passed to it. Calls to it sit behind bounds checks, and a placement resolves these symbols through its symbol map like any other address.
--
--   The statements quantify over every multi-block placement that the `Arm64` model accepts (`Obj.MultiPlacedLib.Placeable`): each block may sit anywhere the relocations reach, independently of the others, so no load address is fixed.
-- source:
--   x25519-dalek 3.0.0 (https://crates.io/crates/x25519-dalek/3.0.0), the 19 functions and 3 read-only constants of x25519-dalek 3.0.0 and curve25519-dalek 5.0.0 reached from StaticSecret::diffie_hellman and PublicKey::from, compiled for AArch64 Linux by rustc 1.98.1 in libsignal and exported by google-deepmind/code-verification @ 8c8d371f (LibsignalBinary/Proofs/HookDischarge/CryptoX25519)

